"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Every repair trade is two jobs: fixing the thing, and the conversation at the counter. The second one decides whether you get to do the first.</p>

        <p>You run the repair side of a bike shop — though everything in this lesson works word-for-word if your counter takes cars, boilers, phones or shoes instead. It's Tuesday morning, and in comes Deb: friendly, fast-talking, holding her commuter bike with the expression of someone describing a mystery illness.</p>

        <p>The customer's version of a fault is never the fault. It's a story — "a sort of clicking, but only sometimes, and my brother-in-law reckons it's the gears." Your first job is to get the real symptoms out of the story without making her feel silly. Your second is the quote — honest about what you don't know yet, with no surprises later. Your third, and it's the one that builds a business: telling the truth even when it costs you money. "Not worth fixing" loses you thirty dollars today and wins you a customer for ten years.</p>

        <p>And underneath it all, the immigrant tradesman's daily test: staying in charge of the conversation when the customer talks at full local speed. Asking someone to slow down feels like weakness. Done right, it's the opposite — it's what the expert in the room sounds like.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Think of a tradesperson you trust completely. What did they SAY — not do — that won you?</li>
            <li>Why does "it's not worth fixing" make a customer trust you more, when it's an admission you can't help?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>The one in the apron. Knows machines better than anyone in the room; knows English second. In charge of the counter — when the phrases hold.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Deb</b><span>Friendly, quick, talks like an auctioneer. Brings one bike with a mystery noise, one question about her kid's old bike, and a brother-in-law with theories.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and watch two things: how the vague story becomes a diagnosis — question by question, never a lecture — and the two places You says something that costs money in the short term and builds the business in the long term.</div>""",

