"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>In 1901, sponge divers sheltering off the Greek island of Antikythera found an ancient shipwreck — statues, coins, and one corroded lump of bronze the size of a shoebox that nobody thought much of. It sat in a museum drawer while the treasure got the attention. Then someone noticed it had gears.</p>
            <p>It took a hundred years of patient diagnosis — X-rays, then CT scans, generation after generation of experts asking better and better questions of the same broken machine — to understand what it was: a two-thousand-year-old mechanical computer that predicted eclipses and tracked the planets, more sophisticated than anything built for the next fourteen centuries. Nobody could ask its maker. The machine had to be talked through what it was doing, one gear at a time.</p>
            <p>Every broken thing that lands on a counter is a small Antikythera: it can't tell you what's wrong, so the skill is in the questions — what does it do, when does it do it, what does that rule out. The divers found the lump. The askers found the computer.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_the_antikythera_mechanism.html">⚙️ <span>Want the full story? The wreck, the drawer, and the two-thousand-year-old computer…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>The mechanism sat ignored in a drawer because it looked like junk. What's the best thing you've ever found — or fixed — that everyone else had written off?</li>
            <li>A hundred years of better questions beat two thousand years of silence. In your trade, what's the question that separates the real experts from the parts-swappers?</li>
        </ol>""",
